Chapter: NetworkEffect
  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites Net chapter HttpFetch
  cites Net chapter HttpClient
  cites Foreword chapter Result

 The Network effect has an implementation, and this is the test that
 says so without a network.

 fetch, post and resolve-dns are definitions in Net chapter HttpFetch.
 They were effect operations that nothing
 implemented: a call type-checked clean and died at the end of emit
 with CDX2040. That failure was invisible to the battery, because no
 test called one. This one does -- resolve-dns on a dotted quad takes
 the fast path and never touches the wire, so it runs headless, and it
 still has to link. If the implementation is ever removed the call
 stops resolving and this test stops compiling, which is the point.

 The rest is known-answer: the URL parse, the header framing, and the
 declared content length. The length is what tells a complete body
 from a truncated one, so it is worth pinning to a fixture.

 The live half -- a real GET and POST against a real server, and a
 name resolved on the wire -- cannot run here, because the battery
 starts no server. It is exercised by hand; see docs/PM/BACKLOG.md 1.7.

 We say:

Section: A Recorded Response

 A response as it arrives off the wire: ASCII, CRLF-delimited, with a
 declared length. CR and LF are written as their wire codes because
 a Codex string literal has no escape for them.

  fixture : List Integer
  fixture =
   http-to-ascii "HTTP/1.0 200 OK" & [13, 10] & http-to-ascii "Content-Type: text/plain" & [13, 10] & http-to-ascii "Content-Length: 14" & [13, 10] & [13, 10] & http-to-ascii "codex-fetch-ok"

  url : Text = "http://api.example.com:8443/v1/status"

Section: Body

  opening : [Console, Network.Read, Network.Write] Nothing
  opening = act
    print-line-uni ("host: " & url-host url)
    print-line-uni ("port: " & show (url-port url))
    print-line-uni ("path: " & url-path url)
    print-line-uni ("default-port: " & show (url-port "http://example.com/a"))
    print-line-uni ("default-path: " & url-path "http://example.com")
    print-line-uni ("headers-end: " & show (http-headers-end fixture 0 (list-length fixture)))
    print-line-uni ("content-length: " & show (http-content-length fixture (http-headers-end fixture 0 (list-length fixture))))
    print-line-uni ("complete: " & (if http-body-complete fixture then "yes" else "no"))
    let resp = http-parse-response fixture
    in act
      print-line-uni ("status: " & show (resp.status-code))
      print-line-uni ("body: " & resp.body)
      print-line-uni ("resolve-dns: " & show-result (resolve-dns "127.0.0.1"))
      print-line-uni ("https: " & show-result (fetch "https://example.com/x"))
    end
  end

Section: Reporting a Result

 fetch, post and resolve-dns answer with a Result, so a failure is a
 value the caller can see rather than an empty string it cannot tell
 from an empty body. Both directions are printed here.

  show-result : Result Text Text -> Text
  show-result (r) = when r
   is Ok (v) -> "Ok " & v
   is Err (e) -> "Err " & e

